      "writtenOn": "2025-06-03T18:23:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Again, if IOMMU driver init failed, we fail to enable DMA protection, so we should fail pKVM initialization?\n\nSo the approach from v1 (call pkvm_iommu_driver_init() explicitly in 2 places: as a part of pKVM initialization, and if pKVM initialization failed) seems more correct to me.",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d373a53d_6d02d350",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-03T20:08:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think failing DMA protection will leave the system in a unsecure state whether or not we fail pkvm initialization. In both cases(DMA failed, pkvm inited and DMA failed, pkvm uninited), we end up in a case where devices will be able to access the memory directly. The only difference being, system will be baremetal or virtualized. I feel this is a cleaner and readable code. I can put a banner saying system is not secure if DMA fails. What do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "0540fe90_839dbffa",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e3e3b45d_52528b9a",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-03T22:26:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think we should be precise in what we mean by \"secure\", i.e. which security model we are talking about. Security within the host (i.e. whether the host kernel is able to protect itself from devices) is not pKVM\u0027s concern. The concern is whether pKVM is able to protect itself and pVMs from devices and from the host that controls those devices.\n\nIf your point is that if we failed to initialize IOMMU once, with pKVM, we don\u0027t need to try it again without pKVM, then I\u0027m ok with it.\n\nMy point is that if we failed to initialize IOMMU, we should\u0027t consider pKVM deprivilege succeeded, i.e. vmx_pkvm_init() should return failure and clear pvmfw [*] (and probably also reprivilege CPUs).\n\nIOW, if pKVM was implemented in such a way that it didn\u0027t need help from the host IOMMU driver to enable DMA protection, and the role of the host IOMMU driver was only to let the host itself use IOMMU, it would be a different story. But that\u0027s not the case, pKVM actually relies on intel_iommu_init() to make the PKVM_HC_IOMMU_SET_RTA hypercall to enable DMA protection, right?\n\n[*] BTW clearing pvmfw is crucial, I think. The bootloader passes pvmfw secrets to the kernel and trusts that the kernel will never have access to those secrets (either via CPU or DMA), except an early time window when the kernel has not exposed itself to all kinds of attack vectors yet. So if we don\u0027t enable DMA protection and yet don\u0027t clear pvmfw secrets, leaving them accessible via DMA by the host, we break this fundamental assumption.",
      "parentUuid": "d373a53d_6d02d350",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0ba32923_71fb8846",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-04T14:05:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ahh ok understood. What I meant was - when the user specifies \"kvm-intel.pkvm\u003d1\", its pkvm responsibility to enforce a secure state and if there is a failure in between, we can\u0027t bail out and leave the responsibility to host kernel. I prefer to prevent the system from booting if there is a failure in between. But as you mentioned in chat, if we need to keep parity with arm64 I am fine with letting the system boot. Allowing a system to boot will be the user accepting a compromised boot and whether or not we disable pkvm, the end state will be the same. we could probably have a tristate for kvm-intel.pkvm (disabled, enabled, permissive) where enable will prevent booting and permissive would boot in a compromised state :-). \n\nI fully agree with you regarding clearing the secrets if iommu init fails. I haven\u0027t tested reprivileging once init_finalize is successful as it does a lot more stuff like setting up EPT and such. It might be trivial and existing code may work, but need to test it. I shall test it and respond. If its not straight forward, I shall clear the pvmfw_rmem in case of iommu failure. v1 also did this, existing code doesn\u0027t do this as iommu init happened before pkvm takes over. What do you think?",
      "parentUuid": "e3e3b45d_52528b9a",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dd5a48a4_ccb5d82c",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/pkvm_host.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1484,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-04T14:43:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I haven\u0027t tested reprivileging once init_finalize is successful as it does a lot more stuff like setting up EPT and such. It might be trivial and existing code may work, but need to test it. I shall test it and respond. If its not straight forward, I shall clear the pvmfw_rmem in case of iommu failure. v1 also did this, existing code doesn\u0027t do this as iommu init happened before pkvm takes over. What do you think?\n\nYep, that\u0027s more or less what I meant.\n\n\u003e we could probably have a tristate for kvm-intel.pkvm (disabled, enabled, permissive) where enable will prevent booting and permissive would boot in a compromised state :-).\n\nOr for example an extra flag to panic if pKVM init fails... We may implement something like that if we want to, though it doesn\u0027t seems to me that there is a need for that. As long as we take care to clear pvmfw secrets, the system is not exactly in a compromised state, it simply is not able to run protected VMs, that\u0027s it. (And if we are worried specifically about IOMMU driver failure and thus complete lack of DMA isolation, independently of pKVM, then such a forced panic should be a responsibility of the IOMMU driver, not pKVM, right?)",
      "parentUuid": "0ba32923_71fb8846",
      "revId": "77f89721a5cee8cf141d23728207f65845eed9f6",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
